Upstream: open issue sequoia-pgp/sequoia#1246 (by nwalfield, 2026-05-11) discusses turning parser
assert failures into errors, the direction of this patch; no MR yet.

BufferedReader::steal asserted that data_consume_hard returned at least `amount` bytes. Return an
UnexpectedEof error instead when a reader returns a short buffer. This is defensive: the only
known way to reach the assert was the 32-bit subpacket length wrap, and
fix-subpacket-len-overflow.patch and fix-rawcert-body-len-overflow.patch each prevent it on their
own. With the rest of the series applied, none of 645k distinct crash inputs collected by
fuzzing the openpgp fuzz targets reaches the assert without this patch (native x86_64 and i686
release builds). No test: no reader is known to break the contract.

diff --git a/buffered-reader/src/lib.rs b/buffered-reader/src/lib.rs
index 409d6ea4..bdc5fb9f 100644
--- a/buffered-reader/src/lib.rs
+++ b/buffered-reader/src/lib.rs
@@ -822,12 +822,14 @@ pub trait BufferedReader<C> : io::Read + fmt::Debug + fmt::Display + Send + Sync
     /// [`BufferedReader`] implementations may optimize this to avoid a
     /// copy by directly returning the internal buffer.
     fn steal(&mut self, amount: usize) -> Result<Vec<u8>, std::io::Error> {
-        let mut data = self.data_consume_hard(amount)?;
-        assert!(data.len() >= amount);
-        if data.len() > amount {
-            data = &data[..amount];
+        let data = self.data_consume_hard(amount)?;
+        if data.len() < amount {
+            return Err(Error::new(
+                ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof,
+                "short read in BufferedReader::steal",
+            ));
         }
-        Ok(data.to_vec())
+        Ok(data[..amount].to_vec())
     }
 
